from pathlib import Path
import json,base64,zipfile,hashlib
import numpy as np,pandas as pd
import matplotlib
matplotlib.use('Agg')
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;d=np.load(P/'rsa_distribution_data.npz');q,u,s,v=[d[k] for k in ['q','u','s','v']];m=pd.read_csv(P/'distribution_descriptors.csv',index_col=0);r=json.loads((P/'validation_results.json').read_text());aa=d['order']
X=np.column_stack([np.ones(20),u[:,:2]]);asym=q[:,94]+q[:,4]-2*q[:,49];res=asym-X@np.linalg.lstsq(X,asym,rcond=None)[0];partial=float(np.corrcoef(res,u[:,2])[0,1]);raw=float(np.corrcoef(asym,u[:,2])[0,1])
Xms=np.column_stack([np.ones(20),m[['mean','sd']].values]);a=asym-Xms@np.linalg.lstsq(Xms,asym,rcond=None)[0];b=u[:,2]-Xms@np.linalg.lstsq(Xms,u[:,2],rcond=None)[0];rms=float(np.corrcoef(a,b)[0,1])
pd.DataFrame({'aa':aa,'RSA_PC3':u[:,2],'upper_tail_Q95_Q50':q[:,94]-q[:,49],'lower_tail_Q50_Q05':q[:,49]-q[:,4],'tail_difference':asym,'tail_difference_residual_PC1_PC2':res}).to_csv(P/'PC3_tail_relationship.csv',index=False)
pd.DataFrame({'quantile_probability':np.arange(1,100)/100,'PC1_loading':v[0],'PC2_loading':v[1],'PC3_loading':v[2]}).to_csv(P/'quantile_loadings.csv',index=False)
fig,ax=plt.subplots(1,2,figsize=(11,4.5),layout='constrained');ax[0].plot(np.arange(1,100),v[2],color='#245779');ax[0].axhline(0,color='gray',lw=.6);ax[0].set(xlabel='Quantile of chain-mean RSA (%)',ylabel='RSA PC3 loading',title='PC3 changes the balance of the two tails')
ax[1].scatter(u[:,2],res,color='#245779',s=16)
for i,c in enumerate(aa):ax[1].annotate(c,(u[i,2],res[i]),xytext=(3,3),textcoords='offset points',fontsize=9)
ax[1].set(xlabel='RSA PC3 score (unit norm)',ylabel='Tail difference after removing PC1 and PC2',title=f'Residual tail asymmetry: r = {partial:.3f}')
fig.savefig(P/'RSA_PC3_interpretation.png',dpi=180);plt.close(fig)
variants=pd.DataFrame(r['variants']).T;props=pd.read_csv(P/'AAindex_PC3_correlations.csv');top=props[props.selected_intrinsic].head(8)[['accession','description','r_PC3','partial_r_PC3']]
summary={'tail_difference_raw_r':raw,'tail_difference_partial_r_PC1_PC2':partial,'tail_difference_partial_r_mean_sd':rms,'RSA_PC3_variance_percent':float(100*s[2]**2/(s@s)),'RSA_input_sha256':'412039f02ff0fb37730e41eacd1bda6d9defb0922318d927e46088c8a0b147f5','n_chains':len(d['a']),'n_residues':1500829,'interpretation':'Exposure-distribution asymmetry, conditional on dominant location/spread components; no unique physicochemical cause established.'}
(P/'interpretation_summary.json').write_text(json.dumps(summary,indent=2))
text=f'''<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>RSA PC3 interpretation</title><style>body{{font:16px/1.55 system-ui;max-width:1050px;margin:40px auto;padding:0 25px;color:#172b3a}}h1,h2{{color:#164d64}}table{{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13px}}td,th{{padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9e1e6;text-align:left}}img{{width:100%}}.key{{background:#eaf4f2;padding:18px}}</style></head><body>
<h1>RSA PC3: exposure-distribution asymmetry</h1><p>5 October 2026 • Interpretation of the same RSA decomposition used in the intrinsic-AA comparison.</p>
<p class="key"><b>Best supported label: Exposure-distribution asymmetry.</b> PC3 describes the balance between the high- and low-exposure tails across protein chains after accounting for the dominant mean/spread variation. No distinct intrinsic chemical property has been identified as its cause.</p>
<h2>Input and reproduction</h2><p>The complete corrected RSA input contains 1,500,829 residues in 6,342 chains. Each observation is a chain-by-amino-acid mean RSA; each amino-acid type is represented by its 1st–99th percentiles across chains. The resulting 20×99 matrix is column-centered and decomposed by SVD, without standardizing the quantile columns. The new PC3 reproduces the previously saved score vector to numerical precision. Input SHA-256: {summary['RSA_input_sha256']}. A local incomplete copy was detected by its hash and replaced for this analysis with the complete original input; all reported results below use the matching complete file.</p>
<h2>What PC3 changes</h2><p>With the original sign convention, the quantile loading is positive at both ends and negative around the middle: at Q05, Q50 and Q95 the loadings are +0.1967, −0.0640 and +0.1045. Increasing PC3 while holding the other component scores fixed therefore lengthens Q95−Q50 and shortens Q50−Q05. The opposite score does the reverse. The loading also correlates at r=0.916 with the centered quadratic normal-quantile shape, consistent with an asymmetry-like deformation.</p>
<p>Define the tail-balance measure T = (Q95−Q50) − (Q50−Q05) = Q95+Q05−2Q50. Its raw correlation with PC3 is only {raw:.3f}, because mean/spread-related differences dominate the distributions. Regressing T on PC1 and PC2 and correlating the residual with PC3 gives <b>r={partial:.3f}</b>. Directly controlling for mean and standard deviation instead gives <b>partial r={rms:.3f}</b>. The scale-normalized tail-asymmetry measure T/(Q95−Q05) has partial r=0.869 after controlling PC1 and PC2; conventional moment skewness has partial r=0.669. Therefore the narrower label 'tail asymmetry' is more accurate than claiming PC3 is simply skewness.</p>
<p>PC1 corresponds closely to mean exposure; PC2 to spread; PC3 captures an additional quantile-shape contrast. PC3 accounts for {summary['RSA_PC3_variance_percent']:.4f}% of the original matrix variance. A strong conditional correlation should not be confused with a large fraction of total RSA variance.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{base64.b64encode((P/'RSA_PC3_interpretation.png').read_bytes()).decode()}" alt="RSA PC3 loading and residual tail balance">
<h2>Interpretation across amino acids</h2><p>High PC3 scores include M, K, G, E and D; low scores include A, N, Q, H and S. These are residual shape differences, not rankings of absolute hydrophilicity or raw skewness. For example, lysine can have slightly negative raw skewness and still have a positive PC3 score after the dominant exposure and spread patterns are accounted for.</p>
<p>Biological reading: conditional on those dominant patterns, positive PC3 indicates a relatively longer tail toward high chain-mean exposure and a shorter tail toward low chain-mean exposure. This is variation across protein chains, not conformational switching within a protein, not a direct measure of local residue accessibility fluctuations, and not evidence of bimodality.</p>
<h2>Chemical-property search</h2><p>All 553 complete AAindex scales were screened descriptively; the 51 curated intrinsic scales were also assessed as a separate search family. No strong single intrinsic correlate emerged. The strongest was the negative-charge indicator (D/E), r=0.383, or partial r=0.454 after PC1/PC2 removal. Heat capacity was r=0.360. These are much weaker than the size correlations for intrinsic-AA PC2.</p>
{top.round(3).to_html(index=False)}
<p>After 20,000 amino-acid-label permutations and adjustment for the maximum absolute raw correlation among the 51 scales, the strongest intrinsic association has p=0.855. Among all 553 scales the best raw correlation is QIAN880128, r=0.528, with maximum-adjusted p=0.856. Its protein-derived predictor provenance also prevents treating it as an intrinsic chemical explanation. Partial correlations are descriptive and have not received a separate multiple-testing inference. These label permutations are a reference null for a fixed set of related amino acids, not evidence of a random biological population.</p>
<h2>Validation and limits</h2><p>In 100 random independent train/test chain halves, training PC3 correlates with the held-out tail-balance residual (removing training PC1/PC2) at median r=0.889, with a 2.5–97.5 percentile range of 0.793–0.954. This supports a reproducible distribution-shape interpretation. Both quantities still derive from RSA distributions; it does not independently validate a chemical mechanism.</p>
{variants.round(4).to_html()}
<p>The score pattern remains similar when using Q05–Q95 (r=0.983), Q10–Q90 (r=0.971), chains of at least 100 observed residues (r=0.974), or the same 2,994 chains containing all 20 types (r=0.939). It is therefore not explained solely by the outermost percentiles or different sets of chains for different amino acids.</p>
<p><b>Important sensitivity:</b> requiring at least three or five residues of each type in a chain changes the PC3 score vector (r=0.751 and 0.457 respectively), although the U-shaped loading remains similar. Requiring ten or twenty has more severe effects and sharply reduces coverage for rare residues; the twenty-residue condition leaves as few as six chains and is not a reliable comparable estimate. These filters alter both the precision of a chain mean and the biological chain population, so they do not isolate sampling noise. The AA-specific PC3 scores cannot yet be claimed to represent a universal chemical trait.</p>
<p>A moment-matched two-parameter beta benchmark on [0,1.5], determined solely by each amino acid's mean and variance, produces a third linear PC explaining 0.215%, with score correlation r=0.703 to observed PC3. This is a descriptive counterexample, not a validated generative model: bounded distribution geometry can contribute a third linear PC without a third independent underlying parameter.</p>
<h2>Suggested wording</h2><p>“RSA PC3 captures asymmetry of the distribution of chain-mean accessibility, expressed as the balance between upper and lower exposure tails after accounting for the dominant exposure and spread components. Its physicochemical origin remains unresolved, and the amino-acid score pattern is sensitive to the minimum number of residues contributing to each chain mean.”</p>
<h2>Reproduction</h2><p>The accompanying package contains the complete aggregated 6,342×20 chain-mean/count matrices, the 20×99 quantile matrix and SVD, all property-screen results, and scripts. Run validate.py and make_report.py to reproduce downstream results from rsa_distribution_data.npz. To rebuild from residues, put the hash-matching residue_RSA_6908_corrected.csv in input/ and run explore.py first. Python dependencies: NumPy, pandas, SciPy, Matplotlib. Seeds are fixed. This supplement does not modify the earlier intrinsic selection or label RSA PC3 as charge, size, flexibility or another unverified mechanism.</p></body></html>'''
(P/'RSA_PC3_interpretation_report.html').write_text(text)
(P/'README.txt').write_text('RSA PC3 interpretation, 5 October 2026. Read RSA_PC3_interpretation_report.html. Downstream reproduction: OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python validate.py, then python make_report.py. explore.py rebuilds aggregates from input/residue_RSA_6908_corrected.csv. NPZ contains all chain means/counts needed downstream; original residue CSV is not duplicated in this package. See interpretation_summary.json for original input hash.\n')
with zipfile.ZipFile(P/'RSA_PC3_analysis.zip','w',zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED) as z:
 for f in sorted(P.iterdir()):
  if f.is_file() and f.suffix not in ['.zip','.log']:z.write(f,f.name)
